## What I verified

The task's premise was false, and the implementer caught it at Step 0. The task asserted "Nothing anywhere derives or displays a hit ratio" and specified two new functions. Every piece already existed:

• format_cache_stats at src/format/cost.rs:391
• Wired at both doors — handle_tokens (commands_info.rs:579) and handle_cost (:604)
• With the exact denominator the task specified, via upstream yoagent::Usage::cache_hit_rate

Building the specified functions would have created a second statement of a metric that already has one — the duplication defect this repo has rules against.

Evidence I collected rather than assumed:

| Check | Method | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Feature pre-exists & wiring | grep -n format_cache_stats | both doors, pre-diff |
| Production behaviour unchanged | extracted the function body from HEAD~1 and HEAD, diff'd | byte-identical |
| Test count claim | cargo test --bin yoyo format::cost | 103 passed / 0 failed — matches the write-up |
| Register drift paid | wc -l src/format/cost.rs vs register entry | 2539 = 2539 ✓ |
| New version markers have a consumer | cargo test --test doc_version_claims | 5 passed |

The guards are not busywork. The user-visible percentage is computed entirely by an upstream method this repo doesn't own — the same exposure that made `main` red for 31 hours over the sonnet-5 preset. The four pre-existing tests assert rendered percentages, so an upstream denominator change would redden all four with receipts reading as a formatting bug. The new cache_hit_rate_denominator_is_input_plus_read_plus_write derives its expectation from the formula, fails with a message naming the real suspect, and carries an anti-vacuous pair proving the fixture can actually distinguish the two rival denominators.

One note, not a failure: the deliverable shifted from "build a feature" to "the feature exists; pin it and fix the doc that hid it." That's the correct response to a falsified premise, and CLAUDE.md states the limit plainly — "Nothing about production behaviour changed" — rather than dressing docs-and-tests as a shipped feature.
